---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import CalorieCalculator from '../../components/calculators/CalorieCalculator.astro';
import ArticleImage from '../../components/ui/ArticleImage.astro';

const title = "BMR & TDEE Formulas: Mifflin-St Jeor, Harris-Benedict, & Katch-McArdle";
const description = "Understand the science behind calculating your Basal Metabolic Rate and Total Daily Energy Expenditure using top equations like Mifflin-St Jeor, Harris-Benedict, and Katch-McArdle.";

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Which BMR formula is the most accurate?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The Mifflin-St Jeor BMR calculator is widely considered the most accurate formula for the general population. It has been shown to estimate resting metabolic rate to within 10% of true values in most healthy adults."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How does the Katch-McArdle formula differ from others?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Unlike the Harris-Benedict equation or Mifflin-St Jeor, the Katch-McArdle calculator requires you to know your body fat percentage. Because it accounts for lean body mass, it is often more accurate for athletes and very lean individuals."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the difference between BMR and RMR?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "BMR (Basal Metabolic Rate) and RMR (Resting Metabolic Rate) are often used interchangeably. However, BMR is strictly measured under more rigorous conditions (after a 12-hour fast, in a dark room upon waking), whereas RMR is measured under less restrictive resting conditions."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---

<ContentLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />
  </Fragment>

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 md:py-24">
    <header class="text-center mb-16">
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-gray-900 mb-6 bg-clip-text text-transparent bg-gradient-to-r from-blue-600 to-indigo-600">
        BMR & TDEE Formulas Explained
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
        Discover the science behind the most popular equations to calculate your resting metabolic rate and total daily energy expenditure.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="mb-12">
      <CalorieCalculator />
    </div>

    <article class="prose prose-lg md:prose-xl prose-blue mx-auto text-gray-700 bg-white/80 backdrop-blur-md rounded-3xl shadow-xl p-8 md:p-12 border border-gray-100">
      
      <h2>Understanding Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR)</h2>
      <p>
        If you want to <a href="/guides/weight-loss" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">lose weight</a>, build muscle, or simply maintain your current physique, you need to understand how your body burns energy. This starts with knowing your <strong>Basal Metabolic Rate formula</strong>. Your BMR represents the total number of calories your body requires to perform its most basic, life-sustaining functions—like breathing, circulating blood, and cellular reproduction.
      </p>

      <h3>Mifflin-St Jeor BMR Calculator</h3>
      <p>
        Introduced in 1990, the <strong>Mifflin-St Jeor BMR calculator</strong> has become the gold standard in clinical settings. Studies indicate that it is more accurate than older formulas, predicting resting metabolic rate within a 10% margin of error for both lean and obese individuals.
      </p>
      <div class="bg-blue-50/50 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6 border border-blue-100">
        <h4 class="font-semibold text-blue-900 mt-0">The Formula:</h4>
        <ul class="mb-0">
          <li><strong>Men:</strong> BMR = (10 × weight in kg) + (6.25 × height in cm) - (5 × age in years) + 5</li>
          <li><strong>Women:</strong> BMR = (10 × weight in kg) + (6.25 × height in cm) - (5 × age in years) - 161</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h3>The Revised Harris-Benedict Equation</h3>
      <p>
        The original <strong>Harris-Benedict equation</strong> was created in 1919 and was the leading standard for decades. It was later revised by Roza and Shizgal in 1984 to improve accuracy. While still widely used, it tends to slightly overestimate calorie needs, particularly in overweight individuals, compared to Mifflin-St Jeor.
      </p>
      
      <h3>The Katch-McArdle Calculator</h3>
      <p>
        For athletes, bodybuilders, and those who know their body fat percentage, the <strong>Katch-McArdle calculator</strong> is often preferred. Unlike other formulas that rely solely on total body weight, Katch-McArdle uses Lean Body Mass (LBM). Since muscle tissue is highly active metabolically, this formula provides a highly tailored approach to <strong>calculate my resting metabolic rate</strong>.
      </p>
      <div class="bg-indigo-50/50 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6 border border-indigo-100">
        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Formula:</strong> BMR = 370 + (21.6 × Lean Body Mass in kg)</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Calculating Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)</h2>
      <p>
        Once you have your BMR, you must multiply it by an Activity Multiplier to find your TDEE. This represents the total calories you burn in a 24-hour period, accounting for exercise, daily movement, and the thermic effect of food.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full text-left rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm border border-gray-200">
          <thead class="bg-gray-50">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold text-gray-900">Activity Level</th>
              <th class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold text-gray-900">Multiplier</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 bg-white">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">Sedentary (office job, little to no exercise)</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">BMR × 1.2</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">Lightly Active (light exercise 1-3 days/week)</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">BMR × 1.375</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">Moderately Active (moderate exercise 3-5 days/week)</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">BMR × 1.55</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">Very Active (hard exercise 6-7 days/week)</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">BMR × 1.725</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Expert Tips for Using These Formulas</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Track your macros:</strong> Knowing your calories is step one. Learn how to divide them into protein, fats, and carbs for optimal results. Check out external resources like <a href="https://examine.com/nutrition/protein-intake-calculator/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Examine.com's protein guide</a> for deep nutritional science.</li>
        <li><strong>Adjust as you go:</strong> Formulas give you an estimated starting point. Track your weight and adjust calories up or down after two weeks of consistent tracking.</li>
        <li><strong>Be honest about activity:</strong> The biggest mistake people make is overestimating their daily activity level. When in doubt, start with a lower multiplier.</li>
      </ul>

      <hr class="my-12 border-gray-200" />

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="space-y-6 mt-8 mb-12">
        <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mt-0 mb-2">Which BMR formula is the most accurate?</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">The Mifflin-St Jeor BMR calculator is widely considered the most accurate formula for the general population. It has been shown to estimate resting metabolic rate to within 10% of true values in most healthy adults.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mt-0 mb-2">How does the Katch-McArdle formula differ from others?</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">Unlike the Harris-Benedict equation or Mifflin-St Jeor, the Katch-McArdle calculator requires you to know your body fat percentage. Because it accounts for lean body mass, it is often more accurate for athletes and very lean individuals.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mt-0 mb-2">What is the difference between BMR and RMR?</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">BMR (Basal Metabolic Rate) and RMR (Resting Metabolic Rate) are often used interchangeably. However, BMR is strictly measured under more rigorous conditions (after a 12-hour fast, in a dark room upon waking), whereas RMR is measured under less restrictive resting conditions.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</ContentLayout>
